259
00:16:29,920 --> 00:16:34,060
Crane operators lifted the road surface onto the bents,

260
00:16:34,160 --> 00:16:37,200
where three workers would properly align the slabs.

261
00:16:41,900 --> 00:16:45,840
Four slabs laid side by side would comprise the 28-foot

262
00:16:45,841 --> 00:16:47,340
the 28-foot wide roadway.

263
00:16:50,340 --> 00:16:52,340
The contractor did a marvelous job.

264
00:16:52,440 --> 00:16:55,480
They designed all of their own construction equipment.

265
00:16:55,580 --> 00:16:59,780
And some of it was designed uniquely for that project.

266
00:16:59,880 --> 00:17:02,600
This wasn't off the shelf stuff.

267
00:17:02,700 --> 00:17:05,600
The three construction goliaths were marvels

268
00:17:05,700 --> 00:17:08,300
and pushed forward around the clock.

269
00:17:08,400 --> 00:17:13,600
But still, they were no match for nature's fury.

270
00:17:13,601 --> 00:17:17,200
In the spring of 1962, a huge storm

271
00:17:17,300 --> 00:17:22,200
caught construction crews in the bay off guard.

272
00:17:22,300 --> 00:17:24,040
It happened on Ash Wednesday.

273
00:17:24,140 --> 00:17:26,740
That's what they call the Ash Wednesday storm.

274
00:17:26,840 --> 00:17:31,560
Everything is subject to mother nature, I'd say.

275
00:17:31,660 --> 00:17:36,960
And we got caught on one of those occasions.

276
00:17:37,060 --> 00:17:40,260
As a brutal nor'easter blew in off the Atlantic,

277
00:17:40,360 --> 00:17:43,160
contractors frantically radioed their construction crews

278
00:17:43,161 --> 00:17:47,000
on the water, telling them to get to a safe harbor.

279
00:17:47,100 --> 00:17:50,100
But the contractor had the big driver.

280
00:17:50,200 --> 00:17:52,800
He decided that he thought he could ride out the storm

281
00:17:52,900 --> 00:17:57,700
because he could jack it out of the water about 20 feet.

282
00:17:57,800 --> 00:18:02,120
And one of those huge waves got under the barge

283
00:18:02,220 --> 00:18:05,980
and lifted it and set it down, kind of cattywampus

284
00:18:06,080 --> 00:18:10,360
on its legs and collapsed the legs on it.

285
00:18:10,460 --> 00:18:12,020
Though no one was injured, when

286
00:18:12,021 --> 00:18:16,460
When the clouds had cleared, the $1.5 million Big D

287
00:18:16,560 --> 00:18:21,460
lay capsized and half submerged in the Chesapeake Bay.

288
00:18:21,560 --> 00:18:23,160
I'll tell you a funny story.

289
00:18:23,260 --> 00:18:26,960
Somebody say on the radio, where's the Big D?

290
00:18:27,060 --> 00:18:29,280
Then somebody else say, bottom of the sea.

291
00:18:32,580 --> 00:18:34,980
Work on the bridge ground to a halt

292
00:18:35,080 --> 00:18:38,620
as crews scramble to build a new Big D with pieces salvaged

293
00:18:38,720 --> 00:18:39,620
from its predecessor.

294
00:18:42,020 --> 00:18:45,860
Through storms and mishaps, the roadway construction moved

295
00:18:45,960 --> 00:18:47,260
forward.

296
00:18:47,360 --> 00:18:50,760
But in the middle of the bay, crews were embarking on the

297
00:18:50,860 --> 00:18:53,160
most challenging portion of the project.

298
00:18:55,360 --> 00:18:59,880
Next, workers create new real estate in the open ocean, and

299
00:18:59,980 --> 00:19:02,880
engineers build a bridge at the bottom of the sea.

300
00:19:05,980 --> 00:19:08,380
The tops of the trestles on Chesapeake Bay Bridge and

301
00:19:08,480 --> 00:19:11,780
Tunnel are aligned so precisely that across the

302
00:19:11,781 --> 00:19:13,820
across the 17 and a half mile structure,

303
00:19:13,821 --> 00:19:16,040
they only deviate by half an inch.

304
00:19:16,041 --> 00:19:17,780
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

305
00:19:25,440 --> 00:19:28,260
We now return to the Chesapeake Bay Bridge and Tunnel

306
00:19:28,261 --> 00:19:29,280
on Modern Marvels.

307
00:19:32,020 --> 00:19:34,520
The Chesapeake Bay is one of the most heavily traveled

308
00:19:34,521 --> 00:19:36,620
waterways in the world.

309
00:19:36,621 --> 00:19:39,280
Every year, over 6,000 merchant vessels

310
00:19:39,281 --> 00:19:46,280
the shipping channels. We have two shipping channels.

311
00:19:46,281 --> 00:19:49,280
One shipping channel goes up to the Baltimore area, known as

312
00:19:49,281 --> 00:19:52,280
the Chesapeake Channel. The other shipping channel is the

313
00:19:52,281 --> 00:19:55,280
Thimble Shoals Channel, which serves the Norfolk Newport News

314
00:19:55,281 --> 00:19:59,280
area. Norfolk, Virginia, is also home

315
00:19:59,281 --> 00:20:04,280
to the largest U.S. naval base on the East Coast.

316
00:20:04,281 --> 00:20:07,280
Countless warships, including aircraft carriers and destroyers,

317
00:20:07,281 --> 00:20:12,280
called the base home. For the bridge designers at

318
00:20:12,380 --> 00:20:16,620
Sverdrup and Parcel in the late 50s, the US Navy would influence

319
00:20:16,720 --> 00:20:19,620
their design even more than nature.

320
00:20:19,720 --> 00:20:22,320
It was originally proposed to put high level bridges over

321
00:20:22,420 --> 00:20:25,760
these two shipping channels. However, the Navy was quite

322
00:20:25,860 --> 00:20:31,000
concerned about in times of war, those high level bridges could

323
00:20:31,100 --> 00:20:36,740
possibly be sabotaged. The Navy worried that if

324
00:20:36,741 --> 00:20:40,380
debris were to block the shipping lanes, the entire East Coast

325
00:20:40,480 --> 00:20:43,980
Naval force would be trapped in the Chesapeake Bay, rendering

326
00:20:44,080 --> 00:20:48,680
it useless, or even worse, a target.

327
00:20:48,780 --> 00:20:52,780
Because of the US Navy not wanting a bridge across their

328
00:20:52,880 --> 00:20:56,700
main entrance to the Naval base, they said you can't do that.

329
00:20:56,800 --> 00:21:00,100
So we didn't do that and went underneath.

330
00:21:00,200 --> 00:21:05,900
And once it was decided that that would be a tunnel, I then

331
00:21:05,901 --> 00:21:09,140
We moved over to the other crossing, the Baltimore Channel,

332
00:21:09,240 --> 00:21:13,500
and decided the tunnel would be appropriate there also.

333
00:21:13,600 --> 00:21:15,640
Two tunnels would need to be built,

334
00:21:15,740 --> 00:21:18,940
each stretching over a mile in length.

335
00:21:19,040 --> 00:21:22,520
The engineers decided that the best tunnel designed for the job

336
00:21:22,620 --> 00:21:26,720
would employ what is called the immersed tunnel method.

337
00:21:26,820 --> 00:21:32,220
And this involves building sections of tunnel about 300

338
00:21:32,221 --> 00:21:37,320
300 feet long in a shipyard as a large steel shell.

339
00:21:37,420 --> 00:21:41,500
One advantage of this method of tunneling is the fact that the

340
00:21:41,600 --> 00:21:45,300
sections of tunnels, since they draw very little water when

341
00:21:45,400 --> 00:21:47,840
they're towed, can be built far away.

342
00:21:47,940 --> 00:21:51,080
In the case of the Chesapeake Bay Bridge Tunnel, the sections

343
00:21:51,180 --> 00:21:54,040
were built in Orange, Texas.

344
00:21:54,140 --> 00:21:57,820
Each 286 foot long individual tunnel piece

345
00:21:57,821 --> 00:22:04,320
was towed via tugboat 1,700 miles to the construction site.

346
00:22:04,420 --> 00:22:07,060
Remarkably, all 37 of the sections

347
00:22:07,160 --> 00:22:09,860
made the journey through the hurricane-prone Caribbean

348
00:22:09,960 --> 00:22:16,380
Sea and around the southern tip of Florida without incident.

349
00:22:16,480 --> 00:22:18,640
Once the tubes were safe in Norfolk,

350
00:22:18,740 --> 00:22:20,940
contractors poured tons of concrete

351
00:22:21,040 --> 00:22:23,720
to form the interior road surface and walls.

352
00:22:23,721 --> 00:22:29,220
The once buoyant empty metal tube sections, now laden

353
00:22:29,320 --> 00:22:32,120
with concrete, floated low in the water

354
00:22:32,220 --> 00:22:34,960
as they were towed into the bay.

355
00:22:35,060 --> 00:22:38,560
Probably the most challenging construction problem

356
00:22:38,660 --> 00:22:41,080
was sinking of the tunnel tubes.

357
00:22:41,180 --> 00:22:45,380
And they would come out supported on barges,

358
00:22:45,480 --> 00:22:50,080
and they had just enough concrete inside

359
00:22:50,180 --> 00:22:52,180
to create what we call negative buoyancy.

360
00:22:52,181 --> 00:22:54,980
that means that they would sink if you let them sink,

361
00:22:55,080 --> 00:22:58,920
but just barely.

362
00:22:59,020 --> 00:23:01,820
The lowering procedure had to be precise,

363
00:23:01,920 --> 00:23:05,420
a difficult task when dealing with a rough ocean currents.

364
00:23:05,520 --> 00:23:09,460
The tunnel builder's goal was to delicately place the 13,000

365
00:23:09,560 --> 00:23:13,740
ton tube section into a trench 40 to 60 feet beneath

366
00:23:13,840 --> 00:23:14,840
the swirling waters.

367
00:23:18,740 --> 00:23:20,880
The engineers decided to use the environment

368
00:23:20,881 --> 00:23:24,020
the environment to their advantage.

369
00:23:24,021 --> 00:23:27,540
They lowered the tubes at slack tide, the period between high

370
00:23:27,541 --> 00:23:31,820
and low tides, when the currents were nearly still.

371
00:23:31,821 --> 00:23:33,480
And at some point while you was lowering it,

372
00:23:33,481 --> 00:23:36,020
they would send the divers down to sit on those,

373
00:23:36,021 --> 00:23:38,640
on the platforms alongside of the tube.

374
00:23:38,641 --> 00:23:41,280
And they would guide the tube into position.

375
00:23:41,281 --> 00:23:44,920
And they'd bring it in to where the tube would just touch.

376
00:23:44,921 --> 00:23:46,980
They had a template on the tubes

377
00:23:46,981 --> 00:23:50,340
that a driver could drop about a one-inch pin

378
00:23:50,341 --> 00:23:51,500
through these holes.

379
00:23:51,501 --> 00:23:55,580
If the pin went home, it was a good alignment.

380
00:23:55,581 --> 00:23:58,640
Then once the sections are joined up underwater

381
00:23:58,641 --> 00:24:02,580
and concreted together, then the steel bulkheads

382
00:24:02,581 --> 00:24:08,380
are cut out of the way and the final concrete connection

383
00:24:08,381 --> 00:24:12,360
is made inside the tunnel to form a continuous tunnel

384
00:24:12,361 --> 00:24:13,280
structure.

385
00:24:13,281 --> 00:24:18,520
Eventually, the walls are tiled and the ceiling is installed

386
00:24:18,521 --> 00:24:22,560
and the finished work is completed.

387
00:24:22,561 --> 00:24:24,460
To connect the trestle sections,

388
00:24:24,461 --> 00:24:28,340
the ends of each tunnel needed to be anchored into dry land,

389
00:24:28,341 --> 00:24:31,960
a scarce commodity in the middle of the bay.

390
00:24:31,961 --> 00:24:34,960
For the bridge designers, the answer was simple.

391
00:24:34,961 --> 00:24:36,740
Build an island.

392
00:24:36,741 --> 00:24:39,980
The study was done on wave forces with models

393
00:24:39,981 --> 00:24:43,420
determined just what would an artificial island sitting there

394
00:24:43,421 --> 00:24:45,720
in the mouth of the bay be subjected to in the terms

395
00:24:45,721 --> 00:24:47,320
of forces.

396
00:24:47,321 --> 00:24:48,320
And they are tremendous forces.

397
00:24:52,320 --> 00:24:55,620
Barge loads of rock would need to be meticulously placed

398
00:24:55,720 --> 00:24:59,260
to defend against the powerful currents.

399
00:24:59,360 --> 00:25:02,460
With other crews simultaneously working on trestles, tunnels,

400
00:25:02,560 --> 00:25:05,480
and roadway, dredges began construction

401
00:25:05,580 --> 00:25:07,080
on the four artificial islands.

402
00:25:10,220 --> 00:25:12,120
The construction of the islands

403
00:25:12,220 --> 00:25:17,020
was a difficult operation because you can't just

404
00:25:17,021 --> 00:25:22,060
place sand in a bay like this and expect it to stay in one place.

405
00:25:22,160 --> 00:25:28,900
The way it was done was to build what we call toe dikes, rock

406
00:25:29,000 --> 00:25:34,840
berms, around the periphery of the island and then fill up

407
00:25:34,940 --> 00:25:38,200
to the top of those dikes and then build a second dike,

408
00:25:38,300 --> 00:25:40,540
filling up to the top of that dike,

409
00:25:40,640 --> 00:25:45,640
and then progressively until you reach the surface.

410
00:25:45,641 --> 00:25:48,440
roughly the size of Yankee Stadium.

411
00:25:48,540 --> 00:25:53,640
Each island contains nearly 1,500,000 tons of sand

412
00:25:53,740 --> 00:25:57,780
and 300,000 tons of rock, painstakingly gathered

413
00:25:57,880 --> 00:26:02,160
from around the bay and lowered into place.

414
00:26:02,260 --> 00:26:06,600
Once the islands had been prepared,

415
00:26:06,700 --> 00:26:10,340
workers integrated the ends of the tunnel tubes.

416
00:26:10,440 --> 00:26:12,900
But the unfinished islands were extremely vulnerable

417
00:26:13,000 --> 00:26:15,240
to the elements.

418
00:26:15,241 --> 00:26:20,240
We had just set the first tube on the third island.

419
00:26:20,241 --> 00:26:25,240
We had it all concreted in, had rock backfilled around it,

420
00:26:25,241 --> 00:26:29,240
and about a day later, a hurricane hit.

421
00:26:34,240 --> 00:26:38,240
And after the hurricane had passed, we went out to survey

422
00:26:38,241 --> 00:26:41,240
to see if that tube stayed in position.

423
00:26:41,241 --> 00:26:45,660
And our survey showed that it slid down the trench 26 feet.

424
00:26:46,020 --> 00:26:50,400
So there was a lot of discussion to the higher-ups

425
00:26:50,401 --> 00:26:54,140
that, what do we do?

426
00:26:54,960 --> 00:26:56,940
The designers really had to scratch their heads.

427
00:26:57,900 --> 00:27:00,440
It had enough weight in it now that it couldn't be picked up.

428
00:27:01,460 --> 00:27:03,860
The solution was that the island was actually built out

429
00:27:03,861 --> 00:27:06,100
to meet the end of that tube.

430
00:27:07,140 --> 00:27:10,620
Work crews spent weeks fixing what had taken nature

431
00:27:10,621 --> 00:27:13,560
only ours to destroy.

432
00:27:13,660 --> 00:27:15,960
To protect the islands from future storms,

433
00:27:16,060 --> 00:27:18,120
as well as the treacherous currents,

434
00:27:18,220 --> 00:27:21,260
construction crews covered them with a final layer of boulders

435
00:27:21,360 --> 00:27:23,260
called armor stone.

436
00:27:23,360 --> 00:27:25,360
Some weighed over 20 tons.

437
00:27:31,180 --> 00:27:34,120
In their day, the islands were some of the most expensive real

438
00:27:34,220 --> 00:27:38,680
estate in the world, costing roughly $5 million a piece,

439
00:27:38,681 --> 00:27:43,720
or $625,000 an acre.

440
00:27:43,820 --> 00:27:46,820
Technically less difficult, but of equal importance,

441
00:27:46,920 --> 00:27:48,720
two high-level bridges would also

442
00:27:48,820 --> 00:27:50,820
need to be built into the structure

443
00:27:50,920 --> 00:27:53,620
to allow commercial fishing and pleasure boats access

444
00:27:53,720 --> 00:27:57,640
to the eastern side of the bay.

445
00:27:57,740 --> 00:27:59,740
Without the bridges, the smaller boats

446
00:27:59,840 --> 00:28:02,340
would have to travel to the middle of the Chesapeake

447
00:28:02,440 --> 00:28:05,740
and use one of the two main channels.

448
00:28:05,741 --> 00:28:10,640
At a length of 3,793 feet, the North Channel Bridge

449
00:28:10,740 --> 00:28:13,740
would be the larger of the two.

450
00:28:13,840 --> 00:28:16,280
Standing 75 feet above the water,

451
00:28:16,380 --> 00:28:19,320
it would be high enough for small and medium-sized boats

452
00:28:19,420 --> 00:28:20,220
to sail under.

453
00:28:26,620 --> 00:28:29,160
The shortest component of the bridge tunnel complex

454
00:28:29,260 --> 00:28:32,060
would be the Fisherman's Inlet Bridge.

455
00:28:32,061 --> 00:28:35,960
The 475-foot span would allow 40 feet of clearance

456
00:28:36,060 --> 00:28:38,100
to boats using the busy waterways

457
00:28:38,200 --> 00:28:40,300
at the southern tip of the eastern shore.

458
00:28:44,700 --> 00:28:47,100
Underwater tunnels and high-level bridges

459
00:28:47,200 --> 00:28:49,820
had been built before, but never

460
00:28:49,920 --> 00:28:54,620
had such a combination of artificial islands, tunnels,

461
00:28:54,720 --> 00:29:00,420
bridges, and trestles ever been attempted.

462
00:29:00,421 --> 00:29:05,520
The bridge opened to traffic on April 15, 1964.

463
00:29:05,620 --> 00:29:10,060
The construction schedule for the project was quite an

464
00:29:10,160 --> 00:29:14,760
incredible accomplishment. The entire project from the time

465
00:29:14,860 --> 00:29:18,980
it started until the time it was completed was only 42

466
00:29:19,080 --> 00:29:22,580
months. Even in this day and age,

467
00:29:22,680 --> 00:29:24,780
that would be a record.

468
00:29:24,781 --> 00:29:30,780
With a 17-mile connection between Norfolk and the

469
00:29:30,880 --> 00:29:33,780
Eastern Shore complete, the bridge would help bring

470
00:29:33,880 --> 00:29:36,720
prosperity to the southern Chesapeake Bay region.

471
00:29:36,820 --> 00:29:40,720
Next, safety concerns make designers take a second look

472
00:29:40,820 --> 00:29:42,740
at their work.

473
00:29:42,840 --> 00:29:47,840
And the bridge has a close encounter with a Navy cargo

474
00:29:47,940 --> 00:29:48,840
ship.

475
00:29:54,780 --> 00:30:04,567
The Chesapeake Bay Bridge and Tunnel is so large that during construction, surveyors had to take measurements from atop a 110-foot tower to see over the curvature of

476
00:30:04,568 --> 00:30:05,268
the Earth.

477
00:30:05,780 --> 00:30:07,260
Modern Marvels will be right back.

478
00:30:12,660 --> 00:30:16,980
We now return to the Chesapeake Bay Bridge and Tunnel on Modern Marvels.

479
00:30:16,981 --> 00:30:21,020
By the early 1970s, the Chesapeake Bay Bridge and Tunnel

480
00:30:21,120 --> 00:30:24,880
had become a well-used part of everyday life, offering

481
00:30:24,980 --> 00:30:28,920
transportation for the people of southeastern Virginia.

482
00:30:33,020 --> 00:30:35,940
With the bridge's completion, the ferry system had ceased to

483
00:30:36,040 --> 00:30:37,340
operate.

484
00:30:37,440 --> 00:30:41,240
Travelers had become completely dependent upon the bridge.

485
00:30:41,241 --> 00:30:44,580
upon the bridge.

486
00:30:44,680 --> 00:30:47,640
That dependency would become painfully evident

487
00:30:47,740 --> 00:30:51,580
in the early morning hours of January 21, 1970.

488
00:30:55,580 --> 00:31:00,600
During a fierce winter storm, the USS Yancey, a 450-foot Navy

489
00:31:00,700 --> 00:31:04,440
cargo ship, broke from its anchorage just 2,500 yards

490
00:31:04,540 --> 00:31:07,240
west of the bridge tunnel.

491
00:31:07,340 --> 00:31:10,500
As the wind gusting up to 50 miles per hour blew the ship

492
00:31:10,501 --> 00:31:13,460
towards the structure, the crew lowered the anchors

493
00:31:13,540 --> 00:31:15,300
to stop the vessel.

494
00:31:15,380 --> 00:31:18,980
With anchors dragging, the Yancey had nearly stopped

495
00:31:19,040 --> 00:31:22,120
when it impacted the bridge.

496
00:31:22,180 --> 00:31:25,480
But once it had made contact, the relentless wind

497
00:31:25,540 --> 00:31:29,060
caused the ship to continually batter the structure.

498
00:31:29,120 --> 00:31:31,420
Sailors worked frantically in the freezing rain

499
00:31:31,480 --> 00:31:34,320
to moor the ship to the bridge.

500
00:31:34,400 --> 00:31:37,260
But their efforts failed as the trestle section gave way

501
00:31:37,320 --> 00:31:40,500
in a shower of concrete.

502
00:31:40,501 --> 00:31:43,440
The bridge tunnel was closed for 42 days

503
00:31:43,540 --> 00:31:46,640
until they could get the piles and driven

504
00:31:46,740 --> 00:31:49,240
and put these bridge decks on.

505
00:31:49,340 --> 00:31:51,040
Well, being closed over a month and a half

506
00:31:51,140 --> 00:31:52,940
certainly disrupted the lives of the people

507
00:31:53,040 --> 00:31:55,540
on the Eastern Shore.

508
00:31:55,640 --> 00:31:57,580
Other factors began to affect the lives

509
00:31:57,680 --> 00:32:00,060
of regular bridge travelers.

510
00:32:00,160 --> 00:32:03,320
By the late 1980s, the district considered

511
00:32:03,420 --> 00:32:06,300
widening the crossing.

512
00:32:06,400 --> 00:32:08,860
The bridge was only a two-lane facility,

513
00:32:08,861 --> 00:32:12,000
lane in each direction with no shoulder.

514
00:32:12,100 --> 00:32:13,600
Cars that broke down on the bridge

515
00:32:13,700 --> 00:32:16,860
or impatient drivers who risked oncoming traffic

516
00:32:16,960 --> 00:32:21,500
to pass slow moving vehicles were serious safety problems.

517
00:32:21,600 --> 00:32:25,680
The commission realized that a two lane facility

518
00:32:25,780 --> 00:32:29,320
could not handle the traffic safely and efficiently

519
00:32:29,420 --> 00:32:31,280
in the future.

520
00:32:31,380 --> 00:32:34,220
So they began investigating the possibility

521
00:32:34,320 --> 00:32:37,080
of adding additional trestles and bridges and tunnels

522
00:32:37,081 --> 00:32:40,420
to make it a four-liner facility.

523
00:32:40,520 --> 00:32:42,680
The Chesapeake Bay Bridge and Tunnel District

524
00:32:42,780 --> 00:32:47,620
began looking for a company to design the structure.

525
00:32:47,720 --> 00:32:50,220
The difficulty would be tying the new bridge

526
00:32:50,320 --> 00:32:53,420
into the 30-year-old original.

527
00:32:53,520 --> 00:32:55,340
The company with a clear advantage

528
00:32:55,440 --> 00:32:58,440
was Ferdrup Engineering, the builder of the first crossing.

529
00:33:01,080 --> 00:33:04,140
A new generation of engineers and construction personnel

530
00:33:04,141 --> 00:33:08,140
would be called on to create this second parallel crossing.

531
00:33:08,240 --> 00:33:09,440
I'm real proud of my sons.

532
00:33:09,540 --> 00:33:11,780
They followed in my footsteps.

533
00:33:11,880 --> 00:33:16,080
Jim, he went to work for Cerdrup also.

534
00:33:16,180 --> 00:33:18,480
He's been with them for 15 years now.

535
00:33:18,580 --> 00:33:21,100
It was an honor for me to work on this project,

536
00:33:21,200 --> 00:33:23,800
having my father having worked on the original one.

537
00:33:23,900 --> 00:33:27,700
I was born during the construction of the original one.

538
00:33:27,800 --> 00:33:29,100
I don't remember it.

539
00:33:29,200 --> 00:33:31,800
I remember seeing pictures of me at the Toll Plaza

540
00:33:31,801 --> 00:33:35,900
at opening ceremonies for the existing structure.

541
00:33:36,000 --> 00:33:37,440
So it was a real honor and a privilege

542
00:33:37,540 --> 00:33:38,740
to be able to come back.

543
00:33:38,840 --> 00:33:42,540
You don't typically go out and build 17-mile long bridges

544
00:33:42,640 --> 00:33:43,740
across the mouth of a bay.

545
00:33:43,840 --> 00:33:46,740
It's not an everyday occurrence.

546
00:33:46,840 --> 00:33:48,240
But the new engineers would have

547
00:33:48,340 --> 00:33:51,560
to face the same problems as their predecessors,

548
00:33:51,660 --> 00:33:53,560
as well as new complications brought on

549
00:33:53,660 --> 00:33:55,860
by the additional structure.

550
00:33:55,960 --> 00:33:58,060
Both the new and the original crossing

551
00:33:58,160 --> 00:34:00,560
are essentially at the mouth of Chesapeake Bay.

552
00:34:00,561 --> 00:34:03,360
And there's tidal flow in and out of the bay.

553
00:34:03,460 --> 00:34:05,960
So if there is obstruction there,

554
00:34:06,060 --> 00:34:08,700
the water has to flow in and out of the bay faster.

555
00:34:08,800 --> 00:34:10,700
And it goes faster around the obstructions.

556
00:34:10,800 --> 00:34:13,600
And then these obstructions would then cause scour

557
00:34:13,700 --> 00:34:18,620
or digging out of the bay bottom, which would be a problem.

558
00:34:18,720 --> 00:34:22,620
To solve this, engineers would design a buffer zone.

559
00:34:22,720 --> 00:34:26,820
No less than 250 feet would separate the two structures.

560
00:34:26,920 --> 00:34:29,280
Any closer, and the water turbulence

561
00:34:29,281 --> 00:34:32,040
would excavate sand from around the bridge supports.

562
00:34:37,320 --> 00:34:41,660
Construction began on June 16, 1995.

563
00:34:41,760 --> 00:34:45,400
The age-old problems of weather, sand, and currents

564
00:34:45,500 --> 00:34:49,000
still daunted the construction process.

565
00:34:49,100 --> 00:34:51,400
During the wintertime, it's vicious out there

566
00:34:51,500 --> 00:34:54,400
because the water temperature drops into the 40s and 50

567
00:34:54,500 --> 00:34:57,280
degrees, and you get a breeze coming across there that

568
00:34:57,380 --> 00:34:58,740
cuts through you like a knife.

569
00:34:58,741 --> 00:35:01,640
It can be hard to stay warm in the winter,

570
00:35:01,740 --> 00:35:06,440
and it's viciously hot during the summer.

571
00:35:06,540 --> 00:35:08,620
But great strides in bridge engineering

572
00:35:08,720 --> 00:35:10,880
had been made in the 30 years since completion

573
00:35:10,980 --> 00:35:13,320
of the first span.

574
00:35:13,420 --> 00:35:15,220
A majority of the parallel crossing

575
00:35:15,320 --> 00:35:18,400
would consist of low-level trestle.

576
00:35:18,500 --> 00:35:21,600
Again, thousands of piles would need to be driven

577
00:35:21,700 --> 00:35:25,100
to support the structure.

578
00:35:25,200 --> 00:35:28,040
Workers would utilize a new jackup barge, which

579
00:35:28,041 --> 00:35:30,980
was a technologically superior descendant of the Big D

580
00:35:31,080 --> 00:35:33,040
and two-headed monster.

581
00:35:33,140 --> 00:35:35,540
This new barge would possess a digital edge.

582
00:35:38,320 --> 00:35:43,560
And this methodology is the pile dynamic analyzer, or PDA,

583
00:35:43,660 --> 00:35:47,100
which is a combination of computer hardware and software

584
00:35:47,200 --> 00:35:50,160
program that, within a transducer,

585
00:35:50,260 --> 00:35:52,960
attached to the piles, it keeps track of the blows

586
00:35:53,060 --> 00:35:58,040
before it, plus the energy transmitted to the pile.

587
00:35:58,041 --> 00:36:03,740
But the biggest change for the new bridge builders would come in the form of satellite-aided

588
00:36:03,741 --> 00:36:04,740
construction.

589
00:36:04,741 --> 00:36:11,720
Another advantage was the use of GPS, Global Positioning Surveying, which was a lot easier

590
00:36:11,721 --> 00:36:16,020
from what they had to do on the original crossing, which was constructing surveying

591
00:36:16,021 --> 00:36:17,020
tires.

592
00:36:17,021 --> 00:36:23,420
Essentially, there's a transmitter that can be placed on the pile, and it sends the signal

593
00:36:23,421 --> 00:36:28,820
to a satellite and the signal comes back and through a computer interface then can get you the

594
00:36:28,821 --> 00:36:34,789
exact position of that receiver to within a tenth of an inch.

595
00:36:34,790 --> 00:36:37,920
With the use of the pile dynamic

596
00:36:37,921 --> 00:36:46,880
analyzer and GPS, crews on the jackup barge successfully drove, cut, and capped the 2,591

597
00:36:46,881 --> 00:36:50,120
piles for the new structure.

598
00:36:50,220 --> 00:36:53,580
With a price tag just under $200 million,

599
00:36:53,680 --> 00:36:56,620
the parallel crossing mirrored the old structure,

600
00:36:56,720 --> 00:36:59,720
except no new tunnels would be built.

601
00:36:59,820 --> 00:37:03,120
To reduce the cost, the new trestle sections tied directly

602
00:37:03,220 --> 00:37:04,540
into the existing tunnels.

603
00:37:07,740 --> 00:37:10,240
But due to growing traffic concerns,

604
00:37:10,340 --> 00:37:13,340
the future of the crossing will involve another mammoth

605
00:37:13,440 --> 00:37:15,640
construction project, the building

606
00:37:15,641 --> 00:37:18,440
of two new submerged tunnels.

607
00:37:18,540 --> 00:37:21,540
And we've always planned to build those additional tunnels.

608
00:37:21,640 --> 00:37:24,440
It's just that money kept us from doing it.

609
00:37:24,540 --> 00:37:28,080
Our original thought was just to build two tunnels fairly

610
00:37:28,180 --> 00:37:32,700
identical to the existing two tunnels we have at a water

611
00:37:32,800 --> 00:37:37,100
depth of about 55 feet.

612
00:37:37,200 --> 00:37:40,600
But as shipmakers build larger, heavier ships,

613
00:37:40,700 --> 00:37:44,100
engineers must constantly reconsider tunnel depth to allow

614
00:37:44,101 --> 00:37:47,940
to allow the deeper draft vessels enough clearance.

615
00:37:47,941 --> 00:37:50,640
We've taken a look at that, and we can do that

616
00:37:50,641 --> 00:37:57,340
by building a four-lane tunnel deeper than the existing tunnel,

617
00:37:57,341 --> 00:38:02,260
tying that four-lane into our existing four-lane trestles,

618
00:38:02,261 --> 00:38:07,760
and then abandoning the original two-lane tunnel.

619
00:38:07,761 --> 00:38:10,300
The commission estimates that the new tunnel complex

620
00:38:10,301 --> 00:38:14,400
cost as much as $900 million.

621
00:38:14,500 --> 00:38:18,000
With no new tunnels being built, engineers and work crews

622
00:38:18,100 --> 00:38:20,780
had to seamlessly combine the existing structure

623
00:38:20,880 --> 00:38:23,580
with the new crossing.

624
00:38:23,680 --> 00:38:26,220
One of the big challenges on building the parallel crossing

625
00:38:26,320 --> 00:38:30,280
was to tie back in to utilize the existing tunnels.

626
00:38:30,380 --> 00:38:33,220
So we had to expand the portal islands out

627
00:38:33,320 --> 00:38:35,400
and allow for traffic to merge back in.

628
00:38:40,300 --> 00:38:43,200
46 months after beginning construction,

629
00:38:43,300 --> 00:38:48,700
the new bridge opened to traffic on April 19, 1999.

630
00:38:48,800 --> 00:38:50,840
Completion of the parallel crossing

631
00:38:50,940 --> 00:38:54,040
ushered in a new era for the Chesapeake Bay Bridge and Tunnel.

632
00:38:57,060 --> 00:39:01,180
Next, after 35 years of service, the original span

633
00:39:01,280 --> 00:39:04,760
receives much needed repairs, and the bridge

634
00:39:04,860 --> 00:39:10,260
undergoes an image overhaul.

635
00:39:11,300 --> 00:39:18,300
Federal law mandates that the entire Chesapeake Bay Bridge and Tunnel complex be inspected every five years.

636
00:39:18,860 --> 00:39:23,500
Because it takes inspectors five years to cover the bridge, the job never ends.

637
00:39:24,100 --> 00:39:25,480
Modern Marvels will be right back.

638
00:39:29,260 --> 00:39:33,660
We now return to the Chesapeake Bay Bridge and Tunnel on Modern Marvels.

639
00:39:33,661 --> 00:39:38,600
In April of 1999, the Chesapeake Bay Bridge and Tunnel

640
00:39:38,700 --> 00:39:41,160
experienced a rebirth.

641
00:39:41,260 --> 00:39:44,900
The structure was now a magnificent four-lane facility,

642
00:39:45,000 --> 00:39:49,000
but that triumph was short-lived.

643
00:39:49,100 --> 00:39:51,320
With completion of the parallel crossing,

644
00:39:51,420 --> 00:39:55,020
district officials immediately closed and began rehabilitating

645
00:39:55,120 --> 00:39:58,820
portions of the 1964 span.

646
00:39:58,821 --> 00:39:59,820
of the 1964 span.

647
00:40:03,660 --> 00:40:06,560
35 years of wind and waves had taken their toll

648
00:40:06,561 --> 00:40:07,560
on the old crossing.

649
00:40:10,000 --> 00:40:12,240
With traffic diverted to the new span,

650
00:40:12,241 --> 00:40:15,400
major repairs began.

651
00:40:15,401 --> 00:40:21,280
During that period of time, we profiled the existing deck

652
00:40:21,281 --> 00:40:26,780
and repaved with asphalt or overlaid the existing deck

653
00:40:26,781 --> 00:40:30,380
to give a better ride. Also during this period of time,

654
00:40:30,480 --> 00:40:34,920
the two major bridges, we completely redecked.

655
00:40:35,020 --> 00:40:40,520
Altogether, our retrofit work was about a $20 million piece

656
00:40:40,620 --> 00:40:44,940
of work. Even after the retrofit,

657
00:40:45,040 --> 00:40:51,080
keeping the 5,171 piles, 34 miles of roadway, and the tunnels

658
00:40:51,180 --> 00:40:55,940
and bridges well maintained is a daily job.

659
00:40:55,941 --> 00:41:00,940
We have a total of 168 employees on the facility at this time.

660
00:41:00,941 --> 00:41:02,940
What do these people do?

661
00:41:02,941 --> 00:41:06,940
They keep the air flow in the tunnel at a constant rate

662
00:41:06,941 --> 00:41:09,940
in order to keep carbon monoxide down.

663
00:41:09,941 --> 00:41:13,940
They do what it takes to make it safe for the traveling public,

664
00:41:13,941 --> 00:41:18,940
from patching potholes to relamping roadway

665
00:41:18,941 --> 00:41:20,940
to doing accident repair,

666
00:41:20,941 --> 00:41:24,940
repairing guardrails and bridge rails across the facility.

667
00:41:24,941 --> 00:41:27,340
that it takes to maintain the infrastructure

668
00:41:27,440 --> 00:41:28,680
or do preventive maintenance.

669
00:41:31,380 --> 00:41:34,180
Tonight, work crews have closed down one lane

670
00:41:34,280 --> 00:41:36,480
in the Thimble Shoals Tunnel.

671
00:41:36,580 --> 00:41:38,780
A specially designed tunnel scrubbing truck

672
00:41:38,880 --> 00:41:41,820
is sent in to clean soot and exhaust from one wall

673
00:41:41,920 --> 00:41:43,820
and half the ceiling.

674
00:41:43,920 --> 00:41:46,620
Once finished, crews will close down traffic

675
00:41:46,720 --> 00:41:50,940
in the other direction to allow the truck's 24 rotating brushes

676
00:41:51,040 --> 00:41:54,240
and high-pressured water jets to clean the other side

677
00:41:54,241 --> 00:41:55,780
of the tunnel.

678
00:41:55,880 --> 00:41:59,040
This piece of equipment will clean our two tunnels

679
00:41:59,140 --> 00:42:01,540
in four 10-hour work days.

680
00:42:01,640 --> 00:42:03,280
We do the work at night as to not

681
00:42:03,380 --> 00:42:05,520
to hinder the traveling public.

682
00:42:05,620 --> 00:42:07,820
And we're proud of the machine because of the speed

683
00:42:07,920 --> 00:42:09,220
and the job that it does.

684
00:42:09,320 --> 00:42:11,560
This is an innovative piece of equipment.

685
00:42:11,660 --> 00:42:15,720
It was basically designed specifically for our use.

686
00:42:15,820 --> 00:42:19,840
And now I know of 14 other tunnels throughout the country

687
00:42:19,940 --> 00:42:23,040
that utilize the same type of equipment.

688
00:42:24,240 --> 00:42:27,240
The cleaning of the tunnel is part maintenance and part

689
00:42:27,340 --> 00:42:30,240
public relations.

690
00:42:30,340 --> 00:42:32,880
The bridge, after all, has become a major tourist

691
00:42:32,980 --> 00:42:38,280
attraction, as well as a point of pride for local residents.

692
00:42:38,380 --> 00:42:41,900
With the opening of the parallel crossing, the Chesapeake Bay

693
00:42:42,000 --> 00:42:45,300
Bridge and Tunnel District began to rethink how the bridge fit

694
00:42:45,400 --> 00:42:48,300
into the community.

695
00:42:48,400 --> 00:42:51,900
The original crossing, the commission had the foresight

696
00:42:51,901 --> 00:42:54,540
on one of the islands to put a restaurant, a fishing pier,

697
00:42:54,640 --> 00:42:55,900
and a gift shop.

698
00:42:56,000 --> 00:42:58,540
And that has been extremely popular with the people

699
00:42:58,640 --> 00:42:59,640
crossing.

700
00:42:59,740 --> 00:43:01,040
Not only the tourists, but the local people

701
00:43:01,140 --> 00:43:02,040
like to stop in.

702
00:43:04,540 --> 00:43:07,440
Unfortunately, motorists were unable to stop anywhere

703
00:43:07,540 --> 00:43:09,560
else on the structure.

704
00:43:09,660 --> 00:43:12,260
During the retrofit, bridge designers decided

705
00:43:12,360 --> 00:43:16,860
to change that, adding a scenic overlook where motorists could

706
00:43:16,960 --> 00:43:21,900
stop and take in the natural beauty of the Chesapeake Bay.

707
00:43:21,901 --> 00:43:25,900
The district also opened the man-made islands to birdwatchers,

708
00:43:26,000 --> 00:43:29,900
offering unobstructed views of the bird sanctuary on the nearby

709
00:43:30,000 --> 00:43:31,900
Fisherman's Island Wildlife Refuge.

710
00:43:37,920 --> 00:43:40,960
But the most successful community affairs program

711
00:43:41,060 --> 00:43:44,760
began as a way of thanking local residents for enduring nearly

712
00:43:44,860 --> 00:43:47,800
four years of constant construction.

713
00:43:47,801 --> 00:43:51,100
construction. As the parallel crossing project

714
00:43:51,101 --> 00:43:57,900
was near in completion, we had an opportunity to open the facility up to the public to come

715
00:43:57,901 --> 00:44:01,800
out and walk on the structure and bike on the structure.

716
00:44:04,180 --> 00:44:10,252
Every year in late October, the district opens the bridge to pedestrians.

717
00:44:10,253 --> 00:44:11,500
And every year,

718
00:44:11,501 --> 00:44:16,500
the crowds grow, coming to take in the beauty and stroll on the

719
00:44:16,600 --> 00:44:20,040
graceful man-made line between the Chesapeake Bay and the

720
00:44:20,140 --> 00:44:25,540
Atlantic Ocean, experiencing firsthand the bridge, which,

721
00:44:25,640 --> 00:44:30,660
like the body of water it spans, has a rich history but continues

722
00:44:30,760 --> 00:44:31,660
to change.

